little learning on the side.

00:11:17.136 --> 00:11:17.969
It should actually feel like,

00:11:17.969 --> 00:11:19.603
okay, this is kind of school.

00:11:21.700 --> 00:11:23.957
We're actually going to
consult with more experts

00:11:23.957 --> 00:11:26.860
and hopefully, as we do
more of these live streams,

00:11:26.860 --> 00:11:30.030
which we're targeting
to do on a daily basis

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:31.500
through this crisis.

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:33.010
I'm hoping to bring some experts in

00:11:33.010 --> 00:11:34.840
who can help us with that, too.

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:39.440
But some of our first stabs at it

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:43.220
would be create incentives, that schedule.

00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:45.203
We'll try to set up like a checklist.

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:47.570
We might even try to
create some frameworks,

00:11:47.570 --> 00:11:49.370
that hey, "If at student is able to do

00:11:49.370 --> 00:11:50.947
X, Y, and Z in a given day,"

00:11:50.947 --> 00:11:55.470
"Here's some treats or some
benefits they might get."

00:11:55.470 --> 00:11:56.303
There might be a little bit

00:11:56.303 --> 00:11:58.430
of quid pro quo with your child. (laughs)

00:11:58.430 --> 00:12:00.570
If they're able to do this really focused,

00:12:00.570 --> 00:12:01.440
good screen time,

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:03.310
maybe they could get a little bit

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:07.130
of less academic screen time.

00:12:07.130 --> 00:12:09.280
There could be other benefits.

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:10.383
And, one of the things

00:12:10.383 --> 00:12:12.440
that we're trying to do
with these live streams

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:16.220
is we wanna be a little bit
of a clearing house, too.

00:12:16.220 --> 00:12:18.160
If you're a parent who has come up

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:21.241
with really good
frameworks, we would love--

00:12:21.241 --> 00:12:23.110
Send it to us.

00:12:23.110 --> 00:12:24.109
- And post.

00:12:24.109 --> 00:12:25.054
- And post, and post.

00:12:25.054 --> 00:12:26.175
- I already see from people, saying,

00:12:26.175 --> 00:12:28.532
"I use Khan Academy all
the time in the summer."

00:12:28.532 --> 00:12:30.697
Or, "I've been homeschooling
with Khan Academy."

00:12:30.697 --> 00:12:33.620
So, if you've developed
some tips or techniques

00:12:33.620 --> 00:12:35.057
about how you motivate your kids,

00:12:35.057 --> 00:12:36.600
please post them and share.

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:37.888
We want our whole community to

00:12:37.888 --> 00:12:39.363
to benefit from your tips.

00:12:39.363 --> 00:12:43.200
- And, email skhan@khanacademy.org.

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:45.320
Email it to us, if we
see really cool stuff,

00:12:45.320 --> 00:12:46.280
we'll be sure to give you credit.

00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:48.361
We'll say, "Hey, this is
what Sadia came up with

00:12:48.361 --> 00:12:50.840
(laughs)for her children."

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:52.070
This is an adaptation of it.

00:12:52.070 --> 00:12:54.050
It seems to be working for her.

00:12:54.050 --> 00:12:55.550
This could good for if you have

00:12:55.550 --> 00:12:57.470
seven- or eight-year old
children or whatever age group.

00:12:57.470 --> 00:12:58.980
So we're going to be working on that.

00:12:58.980 --> 00:13:01.460
What are the motivation mechanisms?

00:13:01.460 --> 00:13:03.590
Another thing that we are contemplating

00:13:03.590 --> 00:13:04.730
and we're trying to figure out how we can

00:13:04.730 --> 00:13:06.360
resource this and do these things.

00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:10.120
Are there ways that we can we can help

00:13:10.120 --> 00:13:10.953
in real time?

00:13:10.953 --> 00:13:11.786
Communicate with your children

00:13:11.786 --> 00:13:13.450
and provide them some frameworks

00:13:13.450 --> 00:13:14.350
during the course of the day.

00:13:14.350 --> 00:13:15.911
So, that's something that
we are very seriously

00:13:15.911 --> 00:13:19.027
looking into our next week and beyond.

00:13:19.027 --> 00:13:22.390
- We've got some more questions gaming in.

00:13:22.390 --> 00:13:23.630
From Jennifer Goldstein.

00:13:23.630 --> 00:13:26.520
How well do the online lessons align

00:13:26.520 --> 00:13:28.270
with the Common Core?

00:13:28.270 --> 00:13:29.596
- So, the lessons,

00:13:29.596 --> 00:13:32.800
Khan Academy has been
building for the building

00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:34.600
around a Common Core for,

00:13:34.600 --> 00:13:36.260
since the Common Core existed.

00:13:36.260 --> 00:13:41.067
So, the lessons that you'll
see in math from K through 12,

00:13:41.067 --> 00:13:43.107
and the lessons you see
on Khan Academy Kids,

00:13:43.107 --> 00:13:45.890
and the lessons you see in
English and Language Arts

00:13:45.890 --> 00:13:49.080
from which are grades two
through eight on Khan Academy,

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:52.930
these are not only aligned
to the Common Core,

00:13:52.930 --> 00:13:55.268
most of them have had external review

00:13:55.268 --> 00:13:58.170
from some of the, even varied
people who helped develop

00:13:58.170 --> 00:13:59.003
the company for us.

00:13:59.003 --> 00:14:01.630
So, they are very, very
much standards aligned.

00:14:01.630 --> 00:14:04.980
If you're in a state that
isn't the Common Core State,

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:08.000
I would say that there's more alignment

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:09.597
than you would think.

00:14:09.597 --> 00:14:11.870
Or any other country that does not

00:14:11.870 --> 00:14:13.086
follow the US standards.

00:14:13.086 --> 00:14:15.840
First of all, we have actually
40 translated versions

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:16.790
of Khan Academy.

00:14:16.790 --> 00:14:18.177
If you go to the website,
you'll see the link

00:14:18.177 --> 00:14:19.992
at the top of banner.

00:14:19.992 --> 00:14:22.440
Spanish is the most built out one.

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:25.914
But our belief, especially,
in a time like this,

00:14:25.914 --> 00:14:29.419
if students are able to engage
in high quality materials

00:14:29.419 --> 00:14:32.640
that are aligned with a rigorous standard

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:34.640
and Common Core is a rigorous standard.

00:14:34.640 --> 00:14:36.610
I'm very confident that they will progress

00:14:36.610 --> 00:14:40.600
in almost any standard that's relevant.

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:42.820
I think the important thing now

00:14:42.820 --> 00:14:44.610
with what's going on with school closures

00:14:44.610 --> 00:14:47.470
is to just keep students learning

00:14:47.470 --> 00:14:48.850
on high quality materials.

00:14:48.850 --> 00:14:50.600
Make sure their reading
skills don't atrophy.

00:14:50.600 --> 00:14:52.260
Make sure their math skills don't atrophy.

00:14:52.260 --> 00:14:54.440
Make sure their writing
skills don't atrophy.

00:14:54.440 --> 00:14:57.070
And, there might be a
glass half full opportunity

00:14:57.070 --> 00:14:59.596
for them to may be even get more practice

00:14:59.596 --> 00:15:02.951
in this time, a little bit more focus.

00:15:02.951 --> 00:15:06.350
- Okay, and I don't know
if Jennifer was a teacher,

00:15:06.350 --> 00:15:08.280
but just in case for all
the teachers out there,

00:15:08.280 --> 00:15:11.060
you can actually put the
Common Core Standards

00:15:11.060 --> 00:15:12.040
into our search box,

00:15:12.040 --> 00:15:13.683
and then, it'll just pull up the videos

00:15:13.683 --> 00:15:16.840
and the exercises associated with them.

00:15:16.840 --> 00:15:21.352
From Jessica Knap, do you
think this would be good for--

00:15:21.352 --> 00:15:23.855
This time would be good for SAT curriculum

00:15:23.855 --> 00:15:25.163
for 10th and 11th graders?

00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:29.100
- Yes, I think this is actually a--

00:15:30.386 --> 00:15:31.770
For those of you who don't know,

00:15:31.770 --> 00:15:33.960
back in 2015, we created this partnership

00:15:33.960 --> 00:15:35.480
with the College Board.

00:15:35.480 --> 00:15:37.890
Khan Academy's official
practice for the SAT.

00:15:38.760 --> 00:15:43.160
And, it's in math, reading and writing.

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:45.330
Today, a majority of all
kids are taking the SATs.

00:15:45.330 --> 00:15:47.230
Use it when you take the PSAT.

00:15:47.230 --> 00:15:48.850
If students get permission,

00:15:48.850 --> 00:15:51.480
their scores can be linked with
their Khan Academy account.

00:15:51.480 --> 00:15:55.250
And, then, it acts as a
diagnostic for Khan Academy.

00:15:55.250 --> 00:15:58.440
And, one thing that we've
always been focused on

00:15:58.440 --> 00:15:59.623
both us and the College Board is,

00:15:59.623 --> 00:16:01.950
we don't view this as just as SAT Prep.

00:16:01.950 --> 00:16:03.920
We view this as preparing
for you for college

00:16:03.920 --> 00:16:05.917
as measured by the SAT.

00:16:05.917 --> 00:16:08.390
And actually, so, the SAT
practice on Khan Academy metric

00:16:08.390 --> 00:16:11.174
is an excellent way to,

00:16:11.174 --> 00:16:12.590
as I said, you can't let--

00:16:12.590 --> 00:16:15.470
To keep those reading comprehension,

00:16:15.470 --> 00:16:17.150
math and the writing skills sharp.

00:16:17.150 --> 00:16:19.000
Because actually,
writing and you can write

00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:22.530
and it's auto graded by another partner.

00:16:22.530 --> 00:16:26.500
And, so even if you're not
taking the SATs in the near term,

00:16:26.500 --> 00:16:29.060
I think if you're a ninth grader or above,

00:16:29.060 --> 00:16:30.880
it can be a great thing to engage with,

00:16:30.880 --> 00:16:32.410
because you're gonna to get practice

00:16:32.410 --> 00:16:34.540
in all of the major domains.

00:16:34.540 --> 00:16:37.300
And, obviously, that will likely

00:16:37.300 --> 00:16:39.467
reflect and improve your SAT score.

00:16:39.467 --> 00:16:42.130
One of the issues in high school normally,

00:16:42.130 --> 00:16:43.400
is that you're so overloaded.

00:16:43.400 --> 00:16:45.173
You have six classes you're taking

00:16:45.173 --> 00:16:46.350
and then, you're doing many hours

00:16:46.350 --> 00:16:47.183
of homework at night

00:16:47.183 --> 00:16:48.360
and then, you have to prepare

00:16:48.360 --> 00:16:50.520
for things like SATs or other tests.

00:16:50.520 --> 00:16:52.610
And, you have to do that on the margin.

00:16:52.610 --> 00:16:54.457
You could view this as an
opportunity to say hey,

00:16:54.457 --> 00:16:55.857
"Look, if you could put in--

00:16:56.810 --> 00:16:57.700
As a high school student,

00:16:57.700 --> 00:16:58.533
if you're able to put in

00:16:58.533 --> 00:17:01.360
even an hour a day on SAT practice,

00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:05.230
maybe 30 minutes on the
English-Language Arts' side,

00:17:05.230 --> 00:17:06.280
30 minutes of math side,

00:17:06.280 --> 00:17:08.640
even that is going to make a massive gain.

00:17:08.640 --> 00:17:10.270
And, as a high school student,

00:17:10.270 --> 00:17:11.760
or parents of high school students,

00:17:11.760 --> 00:17:15.019
I'm assuming there'll be even
more self regulation, (laughs)

00:17:15.019 --> 00:17:16.250
and then, you can spend some more time.

00:17:16.250 --> 00:17:19.500
So, I wouldn't do five hours a day.

00:17:19.500 --> 00:17:20.990
I think that would not be healthy.

00:17:20.990 --> 00:17:22.597
But if you can do sessions of,

00:17:22.597 --> 00:17:25.324
"Okay, I'm going to go
deep for 40 minutes."

00:17:25.324 --> 00:17:26.687
"Then, I'm gonna take a 20-minute break."

00:17:26.687 --> 00:17:28.337
"I'm gonna go deep again for 30 minutes."

00:17:28.337 --> 00:17:29.370
"Take a 20-minute break."

00:17:29.370 --> 00:17:31.600
There is this whole thing
called the Pomodoro technique,

00:17:31.600 --> 00:17:33.980
which is a technique for
avoiding procrastination,

00:17:33.980 --> 00:17:36.130
which we all struggle with many times.

00:17:36.130 --> 00:17:37.287
And it is--

00:17:38.670 --> 00:17:41.350
Set a timer that you're
going to be focused,

00:17:41.350 --> 00:17:43.460
can be 20 minutes, 30 minutes, 40 minutes.

00:17:43.460 --> 00:17:45.597
I definitely won't recommend
more than 40 minutes.

00:17:45.597 --> 00:17:47.400
And then at the end of that,

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:48.970
give yourself another.

00:17:48.970 --> 00:17:49.804
Set the timer again.

00:17:49.804 --> 00:17:51.050
Give yourself a 15-minute break,

00:17:51.050 --> 00:17:53.210
or a 20-minute break, whatever you want.

00:17:53.210 --> 00:17:54.140
Hopefully something healthy.

00:17:54.140 --> 00:17:56.090
Go take a walk, go for a run,

00:17:56.090 --> 00:17:57.950
do something, get outside

00:17:57.950 --> 00:18:00.840
and then, dive back in.

00:18:00.840 --> 00:18:02.450
And if you're going to
do the SAT practice,

00:18:02.450 --> 00:18:04.500
we've actually been doing some analytics.

00:18:04.500 --> 00:18:05.630
It's not fully public yet.

00:18:05.630 --> 00:18:07.850
But the important thing is not to just--

00:18:07.850 --> 00:18:09.780
It's to do as much things

00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:12.010
that are pushing your
learning as possible.

00:18:12.010 --> 00:18:14.770
So, take those full length
practice tests that you can.

00:18:14.770 --> 00:18:16.950
The recommendations, we've
seen some associations

00:18:16.950 --> 00:18:19.230
that the students are able to
follow the recommendations,

00:18:19.230 --> 00:18:20.613
which are always trying to push you

00:18:20.613 --> 00:18:22.870
that they're potentially seeing more gains

00:18:22.870 --> 00:18:25.550
than with the kids are just
randomly jumping around on it.

00:18:25.550 --> 00:18:27.130
So, simple answer is yes.

00:18:27.130 --> 00:18:31.170
I think the SAT practice
is a very good way

00:18:31.170 --> 00:18:34.620
to stay engaged in all the core subjects

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:35.870
in the mastering of them.

00:18:36.780 --> 00:18:39.190
- Okay, and the questions keep pulling in.

00:18:39.190 --> 00:18:43.690
So, now, we have actually
from another Khan,

00:18:43.690 --> 00:18:45.810
a T. Khan. (Saul chuckles)

00:18:45.810 --> 00:18:48.760
Are you planning to
coordinate with teachers?

00:18:48.760 --> 00:18:51.221
We are doing a lot of that in this area.

00:18:51.221 --> 00:18:52.054
- Simple answer is yes.

00:18:52.054 --> 00:18:53.763
We had a webinar yesterday.

00:18:56.950 --> 00:18:57.963
This is just, what you're seeing right now

00:18:57.963 --> 00:19:00.550
is just the very tip of the iceberg.

00:19:00.550 --> 00:19:03.400
We hope to go for this to build momentum.

00:19:03.400 --> 00:19:05.420
So, we hope to, throughout this whole,

00:19:05.420 --> 00:19:07.410
for lack of a better word, crisis,

00:19:07.410 --> 00:19:11.980
we hope to connect with
teachers, support teachers.

00:19:11.980 --> 00:19:14.470
We're trying to talk to
other technology platforms

00:19:14.470 --> 00:19:16.740
and figure out what type of resources

00:19:16.740 --> 00:19:18.173
we might be able to--

00:19:19.200 --> 00:19:20.717
We also want to be a
clearinghouse to find out that,

00:19:20.717 --> 00:19:23.133
"Hey, someone has figured
out, a teacher has figured out

00:19:23.133 --> 00:19:25.330
a good way to virtualize their classroom."

00:19:25.330 --> 00:19:26.870
We want to share it with folks.

00:19:26.870 --> 00:19:29.380
Obviously, not just using
resources like Khan Academy.

00:19:29.380 --> 00:19:31.420
Whatever resources are out there.

00:19:31.420 --> 00:19:33.292
But yes, we want to support
teachers through this,

00:19:33.292 --> 00:19:34.636
parents through this

00:19:34.636 --> 00:19:36.650
and teach and students through this.

00:19:36.650 --> 00:19:38.706
- Can I just give some
practical ways to get started?

00:19:38.706 --> 00:19:41.033
So for teachers, specifically,

00:19:41.033 --> 00:19:44.250
we have a Teach With Khan Group.

00:19:44.250 --> 00:19:45.853
So, go to Teach With Khan.

00:19:46.762 --> 00:19:48.825
You'll be able to connect
with all sorts of teachers

00:19:48.825 --> 00:19:50.384
and they can give you
tips how to get started.

00:19:50.384 --> 00:19:51.337
If you go to our homepage

00:19:51.337 --> 00:19:53.313
and click that little blue ribbon,

00:19:54.210 --> 00:19:56.670
it'll take you to a blog article

00:19:56.670 --> 00:19:58.930
and the link linking to teacher resources.

00:19:58.930 --> 00:20:01.500
So, we'll just have all sorts of videos,

00:20:01.500 --> 00:20:03.370
webinars and resources to help you

00:20:03.370 --> 00:20:05.423
get the best outcome for your students.

00:20:07.750 --> 00:20:12.630
Let's see, we have from Owen Yinka.

00:20:12.630 --> 00:20:13.680
I'm sorry if I am--

00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:16.810
And, is there a solution for remote places

00:20:16.810 --> 00:20:20.660
where internet access
is a major challenge?

00:20:20.660 --> 00:20:23.163
- So, Owen Yinka,

00:20:24.460 --> 00:20:27.190
we don't have a really
elegant solution there.

00:20:27.190 --> 00:20:30.820
Our apps, Khan Academy
Kids does work offline.

00:20:30.820 --> 00:20:34.620
And, our Khan Academy
main app does allow you

00:20:34.620 --> 00:20:37.610
to store some of your content

00:20:37.610 --> 00:20:41.550
but we don't have a really good

00:20:41.550 --> 00:20:43.940
truly offline solution for the main stream

00:20:43.940 --> 00:20:44.773
Khan Academy yet.

00:20:44.773 --> 00:20:46.790
That is one of the gaps,

00:20:46.790 --> 00:20:47.910
obviously around the world.

00:20:47.910 --> 00:20:49.920
We know that's an issue
and in this situation.

00:20:49.920 --> 00:20:50.817
I know a lot of public schools

00:20:50.817 --> 00:20:53.430
who are struggling with this issue of,

00:20:53.430 --> 00:20:55.070
if we close the school,

00:20:55.070 --> 00:20:57.060
which is probably prudent at this point

00:20:57.060 --> 00:20:58.670
for the spread of the virus,

00:20:58.670 --> 00:20:59.980
how do you ensure that all kids

00:20:59.980 --> 00:21:01.180
have access?

00:21:01.180 --> 00:21:03.249
Now, we are fully accessible--

00:21:03.249 --> 00:21:04.082
- There's Kolibri, remember?

00:21:04.082 --> 00:21:05.533
I think one of our--

00:21:06.690 --> 00:21:07.567
If you go to Kolibri--

00:21:07.567 --> 00:21:10.463
