00:21:10.463 --> 00:21:12.070
Kolibri which is an offline,

00:21:12.070 --> 00:21:13.020
so, you can look into that.

00:21:13.020 --> 00:21:17.440
- And, that's spelled K-O-L-I-B-R-I.

00:21:17.440 --> 00:21:18.340
Look into that.

00:21:18.340 --> 00:21:20.860
- Yes, maybe even if
you just search KA LITE,

00:21:20.860 --> 00:21:22.320
it might show up as well.

00:21:22.320 --> 00:21:23.610
And, that one is something,

00:21:23.610 --> 00:21:25.000
I think that's really valuable,

00:21:25.000 --> 00:21:26.020
if you are able--

00:21:26.020 --> 00:21:27.170
Well, you should look into it.

00:21:27.170 --> 00:21:28.614
I actually haven't looked recently

00:21:28.614 --> 00:21:32.077
at what their (mumbles) is.

00:21:32.077 --> 00:21:34.385
But that's I think that's more of--

00:21:34.385 --> 00:21:35.380
Yeah, people should look into that.

00:21:35.380 --> 00:21:36.213
I think that's a little harder

00:21:36.213 --> 00:21:39.541
for an individual to consume on their own.

00:21:39.541 --> 00:21:41.203
But they should try.

00:21:43.557 --> 00:21:45.260
- Our team just wants to remind us

00:21:45.260 --> 00:21:47.530
that you can go download the videos

00:21:47.530 --> 00:21:49.061
on the Khan Academy app

00:21:49.061 --> 00:21:51.240
if you want to watch offline.

00:21:51.240 --> 00:21:53.073
Let's see, we also have,

00:21:54.250 --> 00:21:56.610
from Melinda Wedding,

00:21:56.610 --> 00:21:58.520
are there any plans that help AP kids

00:21:58.520 --> 00:22:01.188
who might be missing the
last few weeks of prep

00:22:01.188 --> 00:22:02.460
before the main tests?

00:22:02.460 --> 00:22:04.680
- Yeah, that's an excellent question.

00:22:04.680 --> 00:22:07.343
Actually, I mean, we
want what we're all gonna

00:22:07.343 --> 00:22:10.100
try to make what we can of this situation.

00:22:10.100 --> 00:22:11.480
You could actually view this

00:22:11.480 --> 00:22:12.550
as a glass-half full

00:22:12.550 --> 00:22:14.500
because traditionally, AP tests,

00:22:14.500 --> 00:22:16.710
which I know, there are
things that students

00:22:16.710 --> 00:22:18.120
want time to prepare for,

00:22:18.120 --> 00:22:19.323
they're rigorous exams.

00:22:20.720 --> 00:22:23.300
It's usually hard as you go onto May.

00:22:23.300 --> 00:22:24.830
Students have all their other classes,

00:22:24.830 --> 00:22:25.663
all their other homework

00:22:25.663 --> 00:22:26.870
and then, between the gaps,

00:22:26.870 --> 00:22:28.270
they have to prepare for AP.

00:22:29.880 --> 00:22:32.290
I don't know where the
College Board is on--

00:22:32.290 --> 00:22:33.990
I know some of the SAT administrations

00:22:33.990 --> 00:22:34.823
have been moved around.

00:22:34.823 --> 00:22:37.061
I don't know what's going to happen in May

00:22:37.061 --> 00:22:38.080
with the AP administration.

00:22:38.080 --> 00:22:39.210
I hope they move forward

00:22:39.210 --> 00:22:40.550
or maybe they can delay it a little bit.

00:22:40.550 --> 00:22:41.383
We'll see.

00:22:41.383 --> 00:22:42.216
We don't know what the world

00:22:42.216 --> 00:22:44.750
is going to be like in
April, much less May.

00:22:44.750 --> 00:22:46.800
But I think it is prudent,

00:22:46.800 --> 00:22:49.730
for kids to assume that
testing is going to happen

00:22:49.730 --> 00:22:51.340
as scheduled.

00:22:51.340 --> 00:22:52.173
And, I think that's where we can

00:22:52.173 --> 00:22:54.210
be really really helpful for folks.

00:22:54.210 --> 00:22:55.820
We have, on Khan Academy,

00:22:55.820 --> 00:23:00.820
AP Calculus, AP Statistics,
AP Biology, AP Physics.

00:23:03.706 --> 00:23:04.790
We are actually in development

00:23:04.790 --> 00:23:06.840
of our new version of our AP Chemistry

00:23:06.840 --> 00:23:08.470
but we have Chemistry,

00:23:08.470 --> 00:23:11.010
the high school and
college level on the site.

00:23:11.010 --> 00:23:13.580
We have AP Microeconomics, Macroeconomics,

00:23:13.580 --> 00:23:14.855
American History.

00:23:14.855 --> 00:23:17.150
AP Government and Civics,

00:23:17.150 --> 00:23:19.350
AP Computer Science Principles.

00:23:19.350 --> 00:23:21.460
And so, I would recommend if
the students are able to put,

00:23:21.460 --> 00:23:23.530
well, now they'll have more time,

00:23:23.530 --> 00:23:26.930
if they are able to put 30 minutes a day,

00:23:26.930 --> 00:23:28.360
40 minutes a day, an hour a day

00:23:28.360 --> 00:23:29.380
into each of the subjects

00:23:29.380 --> 00:23:32.931
that they're plan on taking
the AP test in come May,

00:23:32.931 --> 00:23:34.360
I think they're going
to do very very well.

00:23:34.360 --> 00:23:35.710
As I mentioned earlier,

00:23:35.710 --> 00:23:36.850
what we have isn't just videos,

00:23:36.850 --> 00:23:39.510
it's exercises and these
exercises have been vetted

00:23:39.510 --> 00:23:40.670
by third parties.

00:23:40.670 --> 00:23:41.810
These are the same third parties

00:23:41.810 --> 00:23:43.997
who've helped develop things like AP

00:23:43.997 --> 00:23:45.507
or in so many cases, helped grade the APs.

00:23:45.507 --> 00:23:48.550
So, really is highly rigorous materials.

00:23:48.550 --> 00:23:51.180
So, what I would recommend
is go on Khan Academy,

00:23:51.180 --> 00:23:53.280
every course we have--

00:23:53.280 --> 00:23:54.790
Most of our courses, we
have something called,

00:23:54.790 --> 00:23:56.550
Mastery Enabled.

00:23:56.550 --> 00:23:58.430
What that means is not
only do we have exercises,

00:23:58.430 --> 00:23:59.473
but it's enabled in a way

00:23:59.473 --> 00:24:02.022
that it has game mechanics

00:24:02.022 --> 00:24:04.260
that try to measure
students' knowledge state

00:24:04.260 --> 00:24:05.920
on their current skills.

00:24:05.920 --> 00:24:07.134
If a student is already feeling

00:24:07.134 --> 00:24:09.070
like they've already
covered most of the year

00:24:09.070 --> 00:24:10.470
in their AP class,

00:24:10.470 --> 00:24:12.053
they can go immediately to what's called,

00:24:12.053 --> 00:24:13.230
A Course Challenge

00:24:13.230 --> 00:24:14.590
and take that course challenge

00:24:14.590 --> 00:24:17.210
which should take them about
40 minutes, 45 minutes.

00:24:17.210 --> 00:24:19.080
And, then, they'll see which units

00:24:19.080 --> 00:24:20.630
they're strong or weak in.

00:24:20.630 --> 00:24:22.750
And then, especially, the
units they are weak in,

00:24:22.750 --> 00:24:24.210
they could then, go to each of these units

00:24:24.210 --> 00:24:25.990
and there's things called Unit Tests.

00:24:25.990 --> 00:24:29.027
And then, based on that,
that'll take about 30 minutes,

00:24:29.027 --> 00:24:30.450
and then, they could get a skill breakdown

00:24:30.450 --> 00:24:31.670
of which skills they missed.

00:24:31.670 --> 00:24:33.740
And then, they can go skill by skill.

00:24:33.740 --> 00:24:36.380
And the way that you get level up a skill,

00:24:36.380 --> 00:24:38.360
you can either go to a particular skill

00:24:38.360 --> 00:24:41.477
and get a certain percentage
of them right every time

00:24:41.477 --> 00:24:43.700
and we have a very deep item bank.

00:24:43.700 --> 00:24:46.360
So, you really shouldn't
see a lot of repeats.

00:24:46.360 --> 00:24:49.130
And, then, if you see that skill again,

00:24:49.130 --> 00:24:51.170
and mix a little practice
on the unit test,

00:24:51.170 --> 00:24:52.003
or a course challenge.

00:24:52.003 --> 00:24:54.030
Or what we have now, is
called Master Challenges

00:24:54.030 --> 00:24:56.220
which is based on just repetition review.

00:24:56.220 --> 00:24:57.550
Then, it'll get leveled up.

00:24:57.550 --> 00:24:59.450
So, I'm actually very confident

00:24:59.450 --> 00:25:02.600
that if students who are taking AP,

00:25:02.600 --> 00:25:06.750
put in 30 minutes to an
hour per day, per AP,

00:25:06.750 --> 00:25:09.110
they're actually going
to be quite prepared.

00:25:09.110 --> 00:25:10.140
And, I would to add,

00:25:10.140 --> 00:25:12.760
as you get to the last
few weeks of the AP,

00:25:12.760 --> 00:25:14.247
to augment that with--

00:25:15.130 --> 00:25:17.200
Khan Academy will, I think
prepare you very well

00:25:17.200 --> 00:25:19.506
for the multiple choice portion of the AP.

00:25:19.506 --> 00:25:21.220
For the free response of the AP,

00:25:21.220 --> 00:25:23.060
and most of the APs we
have worked examples of

00:25:23.060 --> 00:25:24.100
free response,

00:25:24.100 --> 00:25:25.423
and so, there I would recommend

00:25:25.423 --> 00:25:29.630
that the student start the video,

00:25:29.630 --> 00:25:30.690
pause it right before

00:25:30.690 --> 00:25:32.490
I'm about to or one of our team members

00:25:32.490 --> 00:25:34.230
is about work through the example.

00:25:34.230 --> 00:25:35.680
And then, work through it themselves

00:25:35.680 --> 00:25:37.007
before the video does it.

00:25:37.007 --> 00:25:39.920
That's the best way to
consume type of content.

00:25:39.920 --> 00:25:44.029
But yeah, I think AP is
very powerful use case

00:25:44.029 --> 00:25:46.735
for the students that are studying for it.

00:25:46.735 --> 00:25:47.568
- Great.

00:25:47.568 --> 00:25:49.349
And, we're getting close to 9.30

00:25:49.349 --> 00:25:51.496
but do you have time for another question?

00:25:51.496 --> 00:25:52.329
- I have time.

00:25:52.329 --> 00:25:53.794
I have a harsh stop at 10 so we can...

00:25:53.794 --> 00:25:56.920
- Okay, let's keep going right now.

00:25:56.920 --> 00:25:58.700
From Gita Gandhi,

00:25:58.700 --> 00:26:01.440
can parents assign things to kids,

00:26:01.440 --> 00:26:03.047
I know I can do so as a teacher,

00:26:03.047 --> 00:26:05.360
but wasn't sure about as a parent?

00:26:05.360 --> 00:26:07.050
- So, there's two things.

00:26:07.050 --> 00:26:12.050
Actually, so, first of all,
what we used to call coaches

00:26:12.460 --> 00:26:15.340
or teacher tools are available to anyone.

00:26:15.340 --> 00:26:17.990
It's not like we check

00:26:17.990 --> 00:26:20.730
that you are a certified
teacher when you--

00:26:20.730 --> 00:26:22.160
It's predominantly used by teachers.

00:26:22.160 --> 00:26:26.220
So, we have both parent
tools and teacher tools.

00:26:26.220 --> 00:26:28.450
The teacher tools are more powerful.

00:26:28.450 --> 00:26:30.080
They're built to be able to support

00:26:30.080 --> 00:26:31.700
a larger number of students

00:26:32.730 --> 00:26:33.563
and things like that.

00:26:33.563 --> 00:26:34.880
And so, from the teacher tools,

00:26:34.880 --> 00:26:37.310
for sure, you can make assignments,

00:26:37.310 --> 00:26:39.240
you can assign specific skills

00:26:39.240 --> 00:26:40.570
you can assign full units,

00:26:40.570 --> 00:26:42.120
you can assign things like unit tests,

00:26:42.120 --> 00:26:44.720
you can assign things like Course Mastery.

00:26:44.720 --> 00:26:47.440
So, I want students to achieve
mastery in this subject

00:26:47.440 --> 00:26:49.210
via a certain date.

00:26:49.210 --> 00:26:52.310
On the parent side, you
can definitely monitor.

00:26:52.310 --> 00:26:54.390
Can you do assignments
from the parent dashboard?

00:26:54.390 --> 00:26:55.880
I would say if you want to do assignments,

00:26:55.880 --> 00:26:57.340
it's safest thing is to
use the teacher tools.

00:26:57.340 --> 00:26:58.173
- Yeah.

00:26:58.173 --> 00:26:59.085
They're free.

00:26:59.085 --> 00:27:00.234
Free teacher.

00:27:00.234 --> 00:27:01.930
- And that's actually where we have

00:27:01.930 --> 00:27:03.410
more sophisticated tools anyway

00:27:03.410 --> 00:27:04.730
and you'll just be a teacher

00:27:04.730 --> 00:27:06.640
with however many kids you have

00:27:06.640 --> 00:27:09.890
as your students. (chuckles)

00:27:09.890 --> 00:27:13.700
- Let's see, from Sadia,

00:27:13.700 --> 00:27:15.170
this is a great question

00:27:15.170 --> 00:27:16.640
for us to start thinking about.

00:27:16.640 --> 00:27:19.370
How can the teacher reach out to students

00:27:19.370 --> 00:27:21.758
during the school closures?

00:27:21.758 --> 00:27:23.223
- Yeah, we're brain storming that.

00:27:23.223 --> 00:27:25.720
- We're in the early
stages of thinking on this.

00:27:25.720 --> 00:27:27.600
I know that at my school district,

00:27:27.600 --> 00:27:29.475
our teachers and superintendent

00:27:29.475 --> 00:27:30.524
were just talking about that

00:27:30.524 --> 00:27:32.924
and we're going to start
off with the easiest

00:27:32.924 --> 00:27:35.063
and most familiar
techniques like group email.

00:27:35.063 --> 00:27:37.510
Parents and the teacher
communicating through emails.

00:27:37.510 --> 00:27:39.240
Some other school systems are using

00:27:39.240 --> 00:27:41.595
Class Dojo where other tips

00:27:41.595 --> 00:27:44.260
or system for that type of communication.

00:27:44.260 --> 00:27:46.030
And maybe this question also means,

00:27:46.030 --> 00:27:49.080
how do you continue to foster
that sense of community

00:27:49.080 --> 00:27:53.470
when you're teaching
online as opposed to being

00:27:53.470 --> 00:27:54.303
in a physical classroom?

00:27:54.303 --> 00:27:57.010
- I think what we're going to try to do,

00:27:57.010 --> 00:27:59.330
and this is all improvisation.

00:27:59.330 --> 00:28:02.643
I think we're all improvising
over the next few weeks.

00:28:05.810 --> 00:28:09.542
So, putting aside all the
things we're hoping to do,

00:28:09.542 --> 00:28:13.040
I think there might be
a convergence here is,

00:28:13.040 --> 00:28:16.197
there is a world where if you do have,

00:28:16.197 --> 00:28:17.299
it could be Google Meet,

00:28:17.299 --> 00:28:18.360
it could be Zoom,

00:28:18.360 --> 00:28:19.610
it could be some of these other tools

00:28:19.610 --> 00:28:21.390
that allows some form
of video conferencing

00:28:21.390 --> 00:28:23.600
and messaging at the same time.

00:28:23.600 --> 00:28:27.080
It would be, I think
pretty cool for teachers

00:28:27.080 --> 00:28:28.367
to be able to communicate students that,

00:28:28.367 --> 00:28:31.510
"Look, I'm going to
available at these times."

00:28:31.510 --> 00:28:33.220
Obviously, it could be
coordinated with the schools.

00:28:33.220 --> 00:28:34.310
Well, I do know some schools,

00:28:34.310 --> 00:28:35.423
my children's school,

00:28:39.380 --> 00:28:40.650
their schedule is going to be 90% same.

00:28:40.650 --> 00:28:42.140
And, they're going to
be using theses types

00:28:42.140 --> 00:28:43.840
of video conferencing tools

00:28:43.840 --> 00:28:46.500
to essentially run their program.

00:28:46.500 --> 00:28:49.390
Now, that's, I would say the best case

00:28:49.390 --> 00:28:51.800
and we know that's a lot
of schools haven't had time

00:28:51.800 --> 00:28:53.610
to plan and do that type of thing.

00:28:53.610 --> 00:28:57.230
And so, I would say, I think
it would be interesting

00:28:57.230 --> 00:28:59.763
if, at the same time, we put out

00:28:59.763 --> 00:29:01.700
what archetype schedules
that could look like

00:29:01.700 --> 00:29:03.690
for students of different age groups.

00:29:03.690 --> 00:29:04.640
It could be really interesting

00:29:04.640 --> 00:29:06.790
if teachers are able to also message

00:29:06.790 --> 00:29:07.623
with their students and say,

00:29:07.623 --> 00:29:09.420
"Hey, if you follow this schedule--

00:29:09.420 --> 00:29:10.638
And obviously, the app schedule

00:29:10.638 --> 00:29:11.660
or whatever you think is appropriate

00:29:11.660 --> 00:29:12.897
for your students.

00:29:12.897 --> 00:29:14.400
"If you follow this schedule,

00:29:14.400 --> 00:29:17.210
I, too, the teacher, I will be available

00:29:17.210 --> 00:29:19.207
at these times at this link."

00:29:19.207 --> 00:29:22.280
At the Google Hangout
or on the Skype link,

00:29:22.280 --> 00:29:24.030
or whatever else,

00:29:24.030 --> 00:29:25.200
and then, you could actually have

00:29:25.200 --> 00:29:27.070
a virtual station rotation model

00:29:27.070 --> 00:29:29.330
because some of your students,

00:29:29.330 --> 00:29:31.537
Okay, it's 9 a.m., the schedule says,

00:29:31.537 --> 00:29:34.874
"Work on your Khan
Academy math for an hour,"

00:29:34.874 --> 00:29:36.510
but the students also know

00:29:36.510 --> 00:29:38.280
that you, as a teacher are available.

00:29:38.280 --> 00:29:39.780
So, if they have any questions,

00:29:39.780 --> 00:29:40.650
they can go to you,

00:29:40.650 --> 00:29:44.569
they can just literally change
the window and say, "Hey."

00:29:44.569 --> 00:29:45.790
It's like office hours.

00:29:45.790 --> 00:29:47.744
So, some of the students
might be working with you,

00:29:47.744 --> 00:29:50.120
and then, you can do deeper problems

00:29:50.120 --> 00:29:51.430
or more challenging problems

00:29:51.430 --> 00:29:54.430
or you might even ping
some students on tools

00:29:54.430 --> 00:29:56.521
like Slack, or some messenger
or something like that

00:29:56.521 --> 00:29:58.240
and say, "Hey, I see
you're having trouble,"

00:29:58.240 --> 00:29:59.727
because you can look at
the Khan Academy dashboard.

00:29:59.727 --> 00:30:01.680
"I see that you're
struggling on this concept,

00:30:01.680 --> 00:30:03.900
why don't you come into my office hours?"

00:30:03.900 --> 00:30:06.020
Essentially, open up a different tab,

00:30:06.020 --> 00:30:07.460
and, "I want to work
with you a little bit."

00:30:07.460 --> 00:30:10.670
I think that could be really really cool

00:30:10.670 --> 00:30:13.810
if students are able to
get that type of support

00:30:13.810 --> 00:30:17.320
while they're working on
tools like Khan Academy.

00:30:17.320 --> 00:30:20.980
And obviously, as a teacher
able to do the lesson plans

00:30:20.980 --> 00:30:22.440
that you were already planning to do,

00:30:22.440 --> 00:30:23.513
that's even cooler.

00:30:24.560 --> 00:30:25.900
And, even in the classroom,

00:30:25.900 --> 00:30:28.270
you think the best use case is,

00:30:28.270 --> 00:30:31.383
teachers are able to do
their traditional lesson plan

00:30:31.383 --> 00:30:33.250
in highly collaborative ways

00:30:33.250 --> 00:30:36.340
and that that's 20, 30% of the time

00:30:36.340 --> 00:30:38.130
that students are able
to personalize learning

00:30:38.130 --> 00:30:39.960
on the platforms like Khan Academy,

00:30:39.960 --> 00:30:42.160
where the teachers are
still being able to see

00:30:42.160 --> 00:30:43.700
what work the kids on working on,

00:30:43.700 --> 00:30:44.980
what they're struggling with

00:30:44.980 --> 00:30:47.020
and where interventions are appropriate.

00:30:47.020 --> 00:30:48.380
But in this type of a context,

00:30:48.380 --> 00:30:53.380
it might make sense to
have more of the student

00:30:53.780 --> 00:30:57.223
self-work but have a lot
of support from teachers.

00:30:58.280 --> 00:30:59.113
- Okay.

00:31:00.260 --> 00:31:03.031
Let's see from Wasi, we have a question.

00:31:03.031 --> 00:31:05.851
I want to home school my children,

00:31:05.851 --> 00:31:08.280
do you have any recommendations?

00:31:08.280 --> 00:31:10.630
I do want to, off the bat mention,

00:31:10.630 --> 00:31:13.753
that we actually have a parent webinar,

00:31:14.960 --> 00:31:16.410
mark their calendars,

00:31:16.410 --> 00:31:18.490
it is next Wednesday

00:31:18.490 --> 00:31:22.290
and knew we will be also recording it

00:31:22.290 --> 00:31:24.525
and make that recording live.

00:31:24.525 --> 00:31:25.358
- Yeah, and--

00:31:25.358 --> 00:31:26.191
- And, you can register.

00:31:26.191 --> 00:31:27.470
Just go to our homepage.

00:31:27.470 --> 00:31:29.560
You can register for the parent webinar.

00:31:29.560 --> 00:31:30.690
- Yeah, so the parent webinar.

00:31:30.690 --> 00:31:33.710
And, the simple answer is
everything we've been talking

00:31:33.710 --> 00:31:34.543
about on this live stream,

00:31:34.543 --> 00:31:36.329
I think is relevant if
you're looking to home school

00:31:36.329 --> 00:31:38.550
your child, depending on the age group.

00:31:38.550 --> 00:31:41.430
As I said, most of the resources--

00:31:41.430 --> 00:31:44.500
Sorry, we have this life from my blinds.

00:31:44.500 --> 00:31:47.813
Most of the resources you need,

00:31:49.070 --> 00:31:51.933
Khan Academy has been
working on it for many years.

00:31:53.510 --> 00:31:55.910
Math for sure, we can help students learn

00:31:55.910 --> 00:31:56.830
at their own time and pace

00:31:56.830 --> 00:31:58.850
starting as early as pre-K,

00:31:58.850 --> 00:32:00.980
with Khan Academy Kids
which goes to first grade.

00:32:00.980 --> 00:32:02.210
And, you can pick up from there,

00:32:02.210 --> 00:32:03.980
all the way through high school

00:32:03.980 --> 00:32:06.540
and early college
mathematics on Khan Academy.

00:32:06.540 --> 00:32:08.280
Students can learn at
their own time and pace.

00:32:08.280 --> 00:32:09.310
We're going to try to put out--

00:32:09.310 --> 00:32:10.740
We already have a lot
of materials out there.

00:32:10.740 --> 00:32:13.180
Things like the webinar are
going to be very valuable,

00:32:13.180 --> 00:32:14.920
for getting a little
bit more tactical help

00:32:14.920 --> 00:32:16.580
but we're going to put a lot of resources

00:32:16.580 --> 00:32:18.600
out there for different age groups.

00:32:18.600 --> 00:32:20.370
What could the schedule look like,

00:32:20.370 --> 00:32:23.880
where one of the Khan Academy
resources can be relevant

00:32:23.880 --> 00:32:26.440
in each of those periods in the schedule

00:32:26.440 --> 00:32:29.090
and what other resources
should folks tap into?

00:32:29.090 --> 00:32:32.023
Books that might be available
online or not online,

00:32:33.520 --> 00:32:37.000
podcasts that can be good
sources of discussion.

00:32:37.000 --> 00:32:38.090
So, we're going to try to support you

00:32:38.090 --> 00:32:39.220
as much as possible.

00:32:39.220 --> 00:32:42.170
The reality is a large chunk of the world

00:32:42.170 --> 00:32:46.273
is being forced to home
school on some level.

00:32:47.420 --> 00:32:50.380
So, I think, we're going to do our best.

00:32:50.380 --> 00:32:53.560
So, yeah, please keep telling
us what questions you have.

00:32:53.560 --> 00:32:56.103
As I said before, my email
is skhan@academy.org.

00:32:57.090 --> 00:32:58.530
We want your questions, your ideas,

00:32:58.530 --> 00:32:59.930
things that would work for you.

00:32:59.930 --> 00:33:03.060
We would love to then,
share with everyone else.

00:33:03.060 --> 00:33:05.017
- Yeah, we really appreciate
what's been rolling in.

00:33:05.017 --> 00:33:07.810
I know that Jeremy who
held our teacher webinar

00:33:07.810 --> 00:33:12.810
last night has already had
a list of 1300 questions,

00:33:12.980 --> 00:33:14.700
between the survey we sent out and the--

00:33:14.700 --> 00:33:16.150
So, what we are doing us,

00:33:16.150 --> 00:33:18.612
we're going through all of their questions

00:33:18.612 --> 00:33:20.180
so that we can create

00:33:20.180 --> 00:33:21.013
and get the experts in

00:33:21.013 --> 00:33:23.100
and create the webinars and the tips

00:33:23.100 --> 00:33:24.290
that will be really helpful.

00:33:24.290 --> 00:33:25.979
So, we're going to keep on doing this.

00:33:25.979 --> 00:33:27.486
- Yeah.

00:33:27.486 --> 00:33:28.380
Are there questions?

00:33:28.380 --> 00:33:32.203
- Let's see, we've gotten
through, let's see.

00:33:33.110 --> 00:33:36.420
From Shaun Rhodes, does
your program use games

00:33:36.420 --> 00:33:37.700
to teach concepts?

00:33:37.700 --> 00:33:40.730
And, he talks about having
a child who's autistic

00:33:40.730 --> 00:33:43.420
at home and wasn't able to make it through

00:33:43.420 --> 00:33:46.100
his multiplication tables until they found

00:33:46.100 --> 00:33:48.709
an online game to master it,

00:33:48.709 --> 00:33:49.576
and then, he was able to master

00:33:49.576 --> 00:33:51.040
those skills (mumbles).

00:33:51.040 --> 00:33:54.830
- Yeah, so, to some degree
is the simple answer.

00:33:54.830 --> 00:33:57.430
I think Khan Academy Kids is the richest

00:33:57.430 --> 00:33:58.970
and that's for younger kids.

00:33:58.970 --> 00:34:03.420
But that's a very rich
game like environment.

00:34:03.420 --> 00:34:06.720
As you get into the
main core Khan Academy,

00:34:06.720 --> 00:34:08.040
we have some game mechanics,

00:34:08.040 --> 00:34:09.310
we have things like energy points,

00:34:09.310 --> 00:34:11.870
master points, avatars.

00:34:11.870 --> 00:34:14.426
The whole mastery mechanics,
you try to level them up,

00:34:14.426 --> 00:34:15.910
you get them fed when you reach

00:34:15.910 --> 00:34:17.050
certain levels on the site.

00:34:17.050 --> 00:34:19.210
So, we have those types of game mechanics

00:34:19.210 --> 00:34:20.377
but it isn't like a--

00:34:22.070 --> 00:34:25.940
There is a spectrum between
game and academic learning

00:34:25.940 --> 00:34:26.811
and we always say,

00:34:26.811 --> 00:34:29.210
we gotta to make sure that
we cover the standards

00:34:29.210 --> 00:34:32.010
and how can we layer as
much game and motivation

00:34:32.010 --> 00:34:33.260
mechanics on top of that.

00:34:34.160 --> 00:34:36.850
But we're constantly
trying to improve that.

00:34:36.850 --> 00:34:38.760
Every year, we try to make improvements

00:34:38.760 --> 00:34:40.570
to make it even more
engaging and motivating

00:34:40.570 --> 00:34:41.810
for students.

00:34:41.810 --> 00:34:43.348
You brought up a really interesting point

00:34:43.348 --> 00:34:45.063
around multiplication.

00:34:46.030 --> 00:34:47.690
Or actually, two things.

00:34:47.690 --> 00:34:50.400
We haven't done official studies

00:34:50.400 --> 00:34:52.050
of Khan Academy with students

00:34:52.050 --> 00:34:53.690
with various learning needs

00:34:53.690 --> 00:34:55.400
whether they're autistic spectrum

00:34:55.400 --> 00:34:58.955
or whether learning needs,

00:34:58.955 --> 00:35:00.730
ADHD or dyslexia,

00:35:00.730 --> 00:35:03.270
but we have had a lot
of anecdotal feedback

00:35:03.270 --> 00:35:06.860
that being able to learn
at your time and pace,

00:35:06.860 --> 00:35:09.470
being able to get as much
practice and feedback

00:35:09.470 --> 00:35:10.380
as you need,

00:35:10.380 --> 00:35:12.270
we do have some game mechanics

00:35:12.270 --> 00:35:14.640
being able to pause, repeat, watch videos

00:35:14.640 --> 00:35:15.590
at different speeds

00:35:17.319 --> 00:35:18.960
is valuable for folks.

00:35:18.960 --> 00:35:20.070
On the point you brought up

00:35:20.070 --> 00:35:24.090
about the multiplication tables,

00:35:24.090 --> 00:35:26.210
I think that's a super valuable one.

00:35:26.210 --> 00:35:28.660
Khan Academy gives practice
on things like that

00:35:28.660 --> 00:35:29.920
but nothing can beat--

00:35:29.920 --> 00:35:32.630
This is just a great bonding experience

00:35:32.630 --> 00:35:33.650
for some of that.

00:35:33.650 --> 00:35:36.663
If your child is second grade,
third grade, fourth grade,

00:35:36.663 --> 00:35:38.280
make sure they have that fluency

00:35:38.280 --> 00:35:41.090
in their core math facts.

00:35:41.090 --> 00:35:42.200
We can give a lot of practice

00:35:42.200 --> 00:35:44.060
on the different standards on Khan Academy

00:35:44.060 --> 00:35:47.240
but there is nothing like
sitting with your mom or dad

00:35:47.240 --> 00:35:48.750
or sibling or teacher

00:35:48.750 --> 00:35:52.620
and making sure you have
your single digit addition,

00:35:52.620 --> 00:35:54.450
math facts really fluent and making--

00:35:54.450 --> 00:35:56.227
And, once you get to
second or third grade,

00:35:56.227 --> 00:35:59.210
making sure you have your
multiplication math facts

00:35:59.210 --> 00:36:00.043
really really fluent.

00:36:00.043 --> 00:36:01.750
I can't tell you how many students

00:36:01.750 --> 00:36:03.060
we've seen who are in fourth grade

00:36:03.060 --> 00:36:03.893
or fifth grade or sixth grade

00:36:03.893 --> 00:36:05.720
or even sometimes high school,

00:36:05.720 --> 00:36:07.630
who you can still tell,

00:36:07.630 --> 00:36:08.950
too much of their cognitive load

00:36:08.950 --> 00:36:12.440
is trying to figure out
what eight times nine is.

00:36:12.440 --> 00:36:14.160
So, they can't focus on the other math.

00:36:14.160 --> 00:36:17.490
And, so you'll be doing
your kids a big favor,

00:36:17.490 --> 00:36:19.100
by just making sure they're super fluent

00:36:19.100 --> 00:36:21.380
at that stuff if they're
not there already.

00:36:23.800 --> 00:36:25.113
So, should we--

00:36:28.060 --> 00:36:29.702
- I think what we can do is wrap up

00:36:29.702 --> 00:36:31.830
because we're going to be doing this again

00:36:31.830 --> 00:36:33.972
as soon as what? Monday? Tuesday?

00:36:33.972 --> 00:36:35.403
- Yeah, I think that's the hope.

00:36:36.830 --> 00:36:37.663
It's been great.

00:36:37.663 --> 00:36:41.340
A lot of great folks have chimed in.

00:36:41.340 --> 00:36:44.410
I know this is a super tough time

00:36:44.410 --> 00:36:45.670
for a lot of folks.

00:36:45.670 --> 00:36:47.060
A lot of folks are trying to figure out--

00:36:47.060 --> 00:36:49.150
I mean, yesterday I was working from home

00:36:49.150 --> 00:36:52.513
and my kids were right next to me.

00:36:52.513 --> 00:36:54.500
- You are on it.

00:36:54.500 --> 00:36:57.803
I know my schools are closing on Monday.

00:36:58.890 --> 00:37:00.670
I do wanna just do a quick shout out

00:37:00.670 --> 00:37:03.293
to Teresa Bryant.

00:37:04.640 --> 00:37:07.825
She just said, "Thank you
Saul this live stream."

00:37:07.825 --> 00:37:10.790
"Because there are so
many parents out there

00:37:10.790 --> 00:37:12.730
feeling panicky."

00:37:12.730 --> 00:37:14.040
I think what's your message

00:37:14.040 --> 00:37:17.900
if a teacher or somebody
is feeling panicked?

00:37:17.900 --> 00:37:20.790
- Yeah, well, Teresa appreciate that.

00:37:20.790 --> 00:37:21.623
I think

00:37:24.580 --> 00:37:26.280
one of the main purposes of starting

00:37:26.280 --> 00:37:27.640
this live stream today

00:37:27.640 --> 00:37:29.803
is that we're all in this together.

00:37:31.230 --> 00:37:33.780
We're parents, everyone's,
a lot of people.

00:37:33.780 --> 00:37:35.659
But we're all trying to figure out this.

00:37:35.659 --> 00:37:39.270
We are in a new--

00:37:39.270 --> 00:37:40.730
Society has never gone through this,

00:37:40.730 --> 00:37:42.390
at least in modern times.

00:37:42.390 --> 00:37:45.330
And, I think for all of us,

00:37:45.330 --> 00:37:47.640
honestly, for me, just to
even connect with y'all

00:37:47.640 --> 00:37:49.107
right now, it's comforting
to me as well (laughs)

00:37:49.107 --> 00:37:51.210
'cause I've been socially isolated

00:37:52.740 --> 00:37:55.263
and so, I think the
best thing we can do is,

00:37:56.240 --> 00:37:58.390
thank God that we have
these types of resources

00:37:58.390 --> 00:38:01.740
so that we can connect
virtually right now.

00:38:01.740 --> 00:38:03.190
But that's what I think it is.

00:38:03.190 --> 00:38:04.361
I think this is going to be the time

00:38:04.361 --> 00:38:06.170
that we're going to see
the best of humanity

00:38:06.170 --> 00:38:07.380
really stepping forward.

00:38:07.380 --> 00:38:11.240
It's our duty to help all of y'all,

00:38:11.240 --> 00:38:13.950
parents, teachers, students
navigate this person.

00:38:13.950 --> 00:38:15.200
We're going to be back on Monday.

00:38:15.200 --> 00:38:17.030
I hope to be able to do this everyday

00:38:17.030 --> 00:38:19.370
and don't be surprised if this grows

00:38:19.370 --> 00:38:21.840
into more types of supports.

00:38:21.840 --> 00:38:23.802
So that as many people as possible

00:38:23.802 --> 00:38:26.210
are not feeling panicky.

00:38:26.210 --> 00:38:28.290
I highly recommend meditation.

00:38:28.290 --> 00:38:29.290
We've actually recorded

00:38:29.290 --> 00:38:30.730
some guided mediations recently.

00:38:30.730 --> 00:38:33.403
Something I've been doing
for the last year and a half.

00:38:35.053 --> 00:38:38.200
I was getting quite
claustrophobic on planes

00:38:38.200 --> 00:38:39.840
and someone recommended meditation.

00:38:39.840 --> 00:38:41.240
That really helped me

00:38:41.240 --> 00:38:42.750
reduce my claustrophobia

00:38:42.750 --> 00:38:45.890
but it has had positive effects,

00:38:45.890 --> 00:38:49.123
well beyond just my plane claustrophobia.

00:38:50.020 --> 00:38:51.370
So, I highly recommend that.

00:38:51.370 --> 00:38:52.870
Especially, with people feeling anxious

00:38:52.870 --> 00:38:55.040
and they're just feeling
overwhelmed by things.

00:38:55.040 --> 00:38:57.080
Just 10 minutes a day, five minutes a day.

00:38:57.080 --> 00:39:00.010
Just sit silently, try
to absorb your thoughts,

00:39:00.010 --> 00:39:01.860
realize that everything's going

00:39:01.860 --> 00:39:03.000
to get better in the end.

00:39:03.000 --> 00:39:05.360
We just have to put one
foot in front of the other

00:39:05.360 --> 00:39:07.343
and just do the next right thing.

00:39:08.715 --> 00:39:09.548
I was telling that to myself

00:39:09.548 --> 00:39:11.040
and my children yesterday

00:39:11.040 --> 00:39:11.873
because everyone is like,

00:39:11.873 --> 00:39:12.706
"What's going on?"

00:39:12.706 --> 00:39:13.756
Just like, look,

00:39:13.756 --> 00:39:15.490
let's just do the next right thing,

00:39:15.490 --> 00:39:16.323
for the next hour,

00:39:16.323 --> 00:39:17.750
the next day, the next week.

00:39:17.750 --> 00:39:19.030
It's all going to work out.

00:39:19.030 --> 00:39:22.465
I think there's going to be
some glass half full moments.

00:39:22.465 --> 00:39:23.573
And, I think we're going to see

00:39:23.573 --> 00:39:24.620
the best of everyone, step forward

00:39:24.620 --> 00:39:26.760
and we'll do what we can on our side.

00:39:26.760 --> 00:39:27.890
So, thanks to everyone.

00:39:27.890 --> 00:39:28.723
- Thank you.

00:39:28.723 --> 00:39:29.790
- For being part of this.

00:39:29.790 --> 00:39:32.323
- Onto the next right thing
for the next hour then.

00:39:32.323 --> 00:39:33.367
- (laughs) Yes, that's right.

00:39:33.367 --> 00:39:34.200
- Okay.

